  "rewrites": {
   "4": {
    "original": "There remains this fact even in a country as accustomed to blows to the head as Mexico, now in a state of near anarchy since a Trump campaign promise, since reneged on apparently, to pay for a U.S.-Mexico border wall, reminded the country's 109 million poor just how \u201cout of order\u201d their nation actually was.",
    "rewrite": "In a country like Mexico, frequently witnessing blows to the head, now sliding into near-anarchy after the Trump campaign's promise to fund a U.S.-Mexico border wall was seemingly abandoned, the reminder was stark: their nation, home to some 109 million poor, truly felt \"out of order.\""
   },
   "1": {
    "original": "Much of the reported impoverishment and lack of economic opportunity is more a product, not of human nature but of long histories of Western nations dominating the rest of the world, and then insisting that non-Western countries adapt to the expectations of globalized \u201cfree markets.\u201d",
    "rewrite": "A significant part of the reported poverty and absence of economic opportunities is not a consequence of inherent human traits but rather of historical Western dominance over other nations, alongside the insistence that non-Western countries conform to the standards of globalized \u201cfree markets.\u201d"
   },
   "7": {
    "original": "On the other hand, throwing Molotov cocktails at the Trebevi\u0107 Fortress, where much of the Jewish resistance against the Third Reich was centred, might well be a step too far in Croatia.",
    "rewrite": "Conversely, besieging the Trebevi\u0107 Fortress with Molotov cocktails, a site of significant Jewish resistance to the Third Reich, could be considered an overreach in Croatia."
   },
   "3": {
    "original": "Until recently, at any rate, right-wing populism in large non-Western democracies was a fairly new occurrence, and so there was something relatively fresh and new to be inspired by.",
    "rewrite": "Up until now, right-wing populism in major non-Western democracies had been somewhat novel, providing a relatively fresh source of inspiration."
   },
   "8": {
    "original": "For many in Croatia, and indeed, after a while, throughout much of the world, demagoguery and mass protest eventually turn to oppression, and often, violence.",
    "rewrite": "After time, many in Croatia and later, across much of the world, saw that demagoguery and mass protest led to oppression, often accompanied by violence."
   },
   "2": {
    "original": "As India has pointed out many times in UN forums, India's historic poverty has for too long been blamed on the country\u2019s own government and not on the West.",
    "rewrite": "India, as it has repeatedly emphasized in UN platforms, has long argued that its past poverty should not be solely attributed to its government but also to the actions of the West."
   },
   "5": {
    "original": "Nor do the left in Latin America and the Muslim World have any shortage of valorous revolutionary iconography to inspire their proletariat.",
    "rewrite": "Neither are the left in Latin America and the Muslim World lacking in courageous revolutionary imagery to motivate their working classes."
   },
   "6": {
    "original": "In both cases, they can remind their suffering citizenry of the many sacrifices of the long war against Western imperialism.",
    "rewrite": "In both scenarios, they can evoke the numerous hardships endured during the protracted war against Western imperialism for their afflicted citizens."
   }
  },
  "final_span": "other hand, there are reasons why Colour Revolutions did not happen in places like Argentina, Chile, Belarus, Pakistan and Burma, despite similar levels of Western repression and corruption in their leadership, all of which is observable today in the United States.\nA significant part of the reported poverty and absence of economic opportunities is not a consequence of inherent human traits but rather of historical Western dominance over other nations, alongside the insistence that non-Western countries conform to the standards of globalized \u201cfree markets.\u201d India, as it has repeatedly emphasized in UN platforms, has long argued that its past poverty should not be solely attributed to its government but also to the actions of the West.\nUp until now, right-wing populism in major non-Western democracies had been somewhat novel, providing a relatively fresh source of inspiration.\nIn a country like Mexico, frequently witnessing blows to the head, now sliding into near-anarchy after the Trump campaign's promise to fund a U.S.-Mexico border wall was seemingly abandoned, the reminder was stark: their nation, home to some 109 million poor, truly felt \"out of order.\"\nNeither are the left in Latin America and the Muslim World lacking in courageous revolutionary imagery to motivate their working classes. In both scenarios, they can evoke the numerous hardships endured during the protracted war against Western imperialism for their afflicted citizens.\nConversely, besieging the Trebevi\u0107 Fortress with Molotov cocktails, a site of significant Jewish resistance to the Third Reich, could be considered an overreach in Croatia.\nAfter time, many in Croatia and later, across much of the world, saw that demagoguery and mass protest led to oppression, often accompanied by violence.\nWith so much of the world's population simply waiting for the comfortable dupes of our global elites to move past the age of fake news, fake charity, fake outrage (including over this election result that gave rise to a global shock and awe electronic reaction) and fake concern to fake emotional solutions of fake compassion, we may be looking at",
  "break_point": null
 },
